{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KjRCLpGiW5ajVCvPzRTLux","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Conventions\n2\t\n3\tThe standards the code of 495 meets. `AGENTS.md` (symlinked as `CLAUDE.md`) carries the project\n4\tspine — stack, commands, architecture, never-do list. How a change is made — the six steps, the\n5\treview, the acceptance run, the landing, the commit messages — is `cycle/README.md`.\n6\t\n7\t## Preflight\n8\t\n9\t**Preflight** is `npm run check`: typecheck, the test suite and the `lint:*` scripts chained\n10\ttogether, under Node 24, the declared floor (`specs/adr/D-50`). It is green before any forward work,\n11\tnot \"green enough for this task\". A defect costs roughly 1× to fix in development, 10× in\n12\tintegration, 100× in production: fix a red gate now.\n13\t\n14\t| Control | Refuses |\n15\t|---------|---------|\n16\t| `lint:code` | what Biome refuses (`specs/adr/D-42`, `D-43`) |\n17\t| `lint:layers` | an import against the direction of `AGENTS.md` § Architecture |\n18\t| `lint:architecture` | a `CMP-*` id claimed in `src/` without a row in `specs/amont/conception-technique.md` §4.1, or an import cycle |\n19\t| `lint:exports` | an export nothing reads outside its module (`specs/adr/D-44`) |\n20\t| `lint:declarations`, `lint:distribution` | a published surface that drifts from `src/` |\n21\t| `lint:story-format` | a story of `specs/stories/` that departs from `cycle/format-de-story.md` |\n22\t\n23\t## Code Style\n24\t\n25\t- One thing per function, one responsibility per module (SRP).\n26\t- Prefer small, focused modules over god files. Split a file when it grows a second responsibility.\n27\t- Give every name a specific, unique meaning. Avoid `data`, `handler`, `Manager`, `Service`.\n28\t- Type everything explicitly. Never use `any`. Never leave a public function untyped.\n29\t- Extract shared logic into one function or module. Never duplicate logic.\n30\t- Prefer an early return over a nested `if`.\n31\t- Throw an exception instead of returning an error code or a boolean sentinel.\n32\t- Delete dead code. Never comment it out — git history already holds it.\n33\t- Boy Scout Rule: leave every file you touch at least as clean as you found it.\n34\t- Write the minimum code that solves the stated problem. No preventive abstraction, no unused\n35\t  configuration layer. Inspect what the dependencies already do before adding a package or writing\n36\t  a capability a maintained library provides.\n37\t\n38\t## Comments\n39\t\n40\t- Keep existing comments on a refactor. They carry intent and provenance.\n41\t- Write why, never what. The code already says what it does.\n42\t- Never write an obvious comment that restates the code.\n43\t- Never leave commented-out code. Delete it and rely on git history.\n44\t- Describe the behavior of the code, never the process that produced it: no ticket, story, review\n45\t  round or session reference in a comment.\n46\t\n47\t## Tests (F.I.R.S.T)\n48\t\n49\t- Run the whole suite headless with one command: `npm test`.\n50\t- Give every new function a test. Give every bug fix a regression test.\n51\t- Name a fake used for external I/O as a fake class, not an inline stub.\n52\t- Keep every test Fast, Independent, Repeatable, Self-Validating, and Timely.\n53\t- Never skip or ignore a test without a written note on what stays unresolved.\n54\t- Test every boundary condition: empty input, maximum, minimum, and the off-by-one case.\n55\t- Assert only through the public interface — return values, contracts, view state. Never assert on\n56\t  private state.\n57\t- A test is written before the code it pins, and fails on the assertion the story states before\n58\t  that code exists (`cycle/README.md` § Les six pas).\n59\t\n60\t## Dependencies\n61\t\n62\t- Inject a dependency through a constructor or parameter. Never reach for a global or a bare import.\n63\t- Wrap a third-party library behind a project-owned port (see `src/ports/`).\n64\t- Keep a dependency at its latest published version whenever Preflight stays green; a caret on a\n65\t  `0.x` version silently locks the minor. `@types/node` tracks `engines.node`, not the newest\n66\t  release (`specs/adr/D-50`).\n67\t\n68\t## Pi is the host, not one dependency among others\n69\t\n70\t495 is an extension of Pi. Pi is what keeps 495 from rebuilding what already exists, and what lets\n71\tit state a fact instead of inferring one. Reach for Pi's own API first — before writing the\n72\tcapability, and before deducing from the outside what Pi can report from the inside\n73\t(`specs/adr/D-55`).\n74\t\n75\t- **Look for the API before building.** A hook, an event, a runtime or a typed result Pi already\n76\t  publishes beats a table 495 maintains, a file 495 parses, or a fact 495 reads out of a package's\n77\t  code.\n78\t- **Read the documentation the pinned package ships.** It is already on disk, at the exact version\n79\t  installed: `node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/docs/`, and `extensions.md` there carries\n80\t  the event list. Prefer it to any URL: <https://pi.dev/docs/latest> tracks the newest Pi, not the\n81\t  one this repository has.\n82\t- **Confirm in the pinned surface before building on it.** `@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent/dist/**/*.d.ts`\n83\t  and the peer packages say what the release ships. What is built on an API names the version it\n84\t  was confirmed against.\n85\t- **Prefer an observed fact to a restated one.** What Pi hands over was measured; what 495 restates\n86\t  is believed, and drifts the day the host changes. Say in the code which of the two a value is.\n87\t- **A workaround names what it replaces.** When no API covers the need, write where the workaround\n88\t  lives what was looked for and not found, so it can be deleted the day it arrives.\n89\t- **Leaning on Pi never bends the layering.** The Pi API is reached through `extension/` or\n90\t  `adapters/pi-worker/` and nowhere else (`AGENTS.md` § Architecture).\n91\t\n